n for the systems  of the population in the region  but millions in tigrai remain out of  reach of aid  as the conflict leaves hunger in its  wake  [Music]  for more i'm now joined by the ethiopian  american author and writer maza  miningista her new novel  the shadow king was shortlisted for the  booker prize the books about italy's  invasion of ethiopia in 1935. welcome to  dw news africa  maza i do want to begin by asking you  about the current conflicts in your  country of birth  the u.n said that the world is watching  the unfolding of a full-scale  humanitarian crisis  how does that make you feel  well you know this has been really  it's a distressing um it's been a  distressing  event to watch this from afar  uh to know that there are entire  groups of people that are being left  destitute  that people's hopes and dreams for their  families  are being destroyed and people  themselves are dying  it's uh it's been a horrible month  um and you know the fact that the world  is watching  it feels like uh this is also  um a part of this war part of everything  that has been happening in 2020  earlier is information versus  transformation  your latest novel the shadow king tell  us more about  your using fiction to tell the story of  how ethiopians fought the italians  during the invasion  uh you know um i i was  interested in looking at what war can  tell us about  uh human beings the human condition  what war can reveal about our fears our  love  our ambitions our dreams the war that  took place in 1935 when  italy invaded ironically italy invaded  and around the region that is being  um that is at in conflict today  so i've been looking at what's happening  in 2020 and thinking very much  about the way that war was fought on the  same grounds in 1935.  now the main protagonist uh uh in in the  shadow king is a woman the  the other main characters are also women  why was this important for you to do  i wanted to think about the fact that  when we speak about  war 